{:edb/db-before-t 1818 :edb/db-after-t 1819 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535131 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:40:07.607Z" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151901 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/11b0a0434116475ce544b737e282fd34f252aa781a72e7bdedf1d868d47087c0.png)\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151901 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139535131 false] [17592186151901 1145 "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/de0e0e242c55d338db39c80ce9b34c4a841aacef15a2368e1c00610ece9d2679.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151901 1145 "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$" 13194139535131 false] [17592186151902 1100 "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/3b0c64bc040716ca8ba42c78a229d1249ec7f80a3a4a584970863b20126c69db.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151902 1100 "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139535131 false] [17592186151902 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/a32e0386507fdc40cc713854dde4ec413c2dfbb4c49a6e758e8690344f08459a.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151902 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535131 false] [17592186151903 1100 "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/3af646f67ee7157e5157ddd1d4296716e859c7d7c7c18d45037219ed4f75606e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151903 1100 "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139535131 false] [17592186151903 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/eaa950b7f0525109f33153dc28218222e1da17d7643281e9ac17776e87503784.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186151903 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535131 false] [17592186152104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332830 13194139535131 true] [17592186152104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332837 13194139535131 true] [17592186152105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332827 13194139535131 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332822 13194139535131 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332844 13194139535131 true] [17592186152696 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535131 true] [17592186152696 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $S^{′}$, the image of $S(-4, \\sqrt{3})$ under the action of $T$?" 13194139535131 false] [17592186152696 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S(-4,\\sqrt{3})$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186152696 1145 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $S:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(15,-1).$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186280929 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332821 13194139535131 true] [17592186283250 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332826 13194139535131 true] [17592186332821 1034 "2x+17y" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535131 true] [17592186332821 1111 #uuid "823c5cce-41be-5175-aa8c-687fc9c7ecfb" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332822 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332823 13194139535131 true] [17592186332822 1089 "q-281113" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332822 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535131 true] [17592186332822 1100 "Consider the line $y=x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-7.$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332822 1113 #uuid "2cbc0409-ba51-5eb8-9d01-7f6a8936dc93" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332822 1145 "First, notice that the line $y=x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,3)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{4-3}{5-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-9 & =x-2 \\\\ x-3y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332824 13194139535131 true] [17592186332823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332825 13194139535131 true] [17592186332823 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535131 true] [17592186332823 1041 "field-1" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332823 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332825 13194139535131 true] [17592186332823 1110 #uuid "5d5d200a-7d4c-5c91-bf17-857db4a6b9ec" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332824 1034 "x+3y" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535131 true] [17592186332824 1111 #uuid "c9048972-fec5-594f-b082-7ab1034f3abf" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332825 1034 "x-3y" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535131 true] [17592186332825 1111 #uuid "4a3abb6d-b0fc-5d60-aeb1-3cf9e6fa29e7" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332826 1034 "x+4y" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535131 true] [17592186332826 1111 #uuid "8eea4c9e-12b0-5728-82a8-520c7e9ddfbb" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332827 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332828 13194139535131 true] [17592186332827 1089 "q-281582" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332827 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535131 true] [17592186332827 1100 "Consider the line $y=2$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=14.$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332827 1113 #uuid "e7b045a1-aff7-5596-b6f8-88fcb7f08dfd" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332827 1145 "First, notice that the line $y=2$ passes through the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(2,4)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1-4}{4-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-3x+6 \\\\ 3x+2y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332829 13194139535131 true] [17592186332828 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535131 true] [17592186332828 1041 "field-1" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332828 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332829 13194139535131 true] [17592186332828 1110 #uuid "d491e40d-6d9f-5765-aa52-f4ce198a18d6" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332829 1034 "3x+2y" 13194139535131 true] [17592186332829 1103 